Let a knowledge translation setting consist of a source knowledge base $\mathcal{K}_S = (\mathcal{T}_S, \mathcal{A}_S)$ and a target knowledge base $\mathcal{K}_T = (\mathcal{T}_T, \mathcal{A}_T)$, each comprising a schema/ontology (TBox) and a set of facts (ABox), together with a mapping $\mathcal{M}$ between them. The mapping $\mathcal{M}$ is a finite set of rules (e.g., source-to-target tuple-generating dependencies, equality-generating dependencies, or GLAV/SPARQL-based correspondences) that translates instance data and queries from $\mathcal{K}_S$ to $\mathcal{K}_T$. Say that $\mathcal{M}$ is valid for $(\mathcal{K}_S, \mathcal{K}_T)$ if every rule of $\mathcal{M}$ uses only predicates/concepts existing in the current schemas and is satisfiable, and consistent if its chase against $\mathcal{K}_S$ produces an ABox that, when added to $\mathcal{A}_T$, does not violate $\mathcal{T}_T$.

Suppose that over time $\mathcal{K}_S$ and/or $\mathcal{K}_T$ undergo a sequence of changes $\Delta_1, \Delta_2, \dots$ (insertions, deletions, and modifications at either the schema or the ABox level), yielding evolved versions $\mathcal{K}_S^{(i)}$ and $\mathcal{K}_T^{(i)}$. After such changes, $\mathcal{M}$ may become invalid or inconsistent.

Devise a general framework for mapping repair and adaptation that, given the original $\mathcal{M}$ and the change sequence, computes an updated mapping $\mathcal{M}'$ such that:
(1) $\mathcal{M}'$ is valid and consistent for the evolved $(\mathcal{K}_S^{(i)}, \mathcal{K}_T^{(i)})$;
(2) $\mathcal{M}'$ is not regenerated from scratch but obtained by incremental modification of $\mathcal{M}$;
(3) $\mathcal{M}'$ preserves as much of the original semantics of $\mathcal{M}$ as possible, formalized via a suitable similarity or distance measure between mappings (for instance, agreement on certain answers of conjunctive queries over $\mathcal{K}_S^{(i)}$);
(4) the query-answering capability of $\mathcal{M}$ over $(\mathcal{K}_S, \mathcal{K}_T)$ is preserved in $\mathcal{M}'$ over $(\mathcal{K}_S^{(i)}, \mathcal{K}_T^{(i)})$ to the maximum extent possible.

Provide: a precise formal definition of repair/adaptation operators on $\mathcal{M}$ for each elementary change type; semantics of "maximal preservation" together with existence and uniqueness results; algorithms for computing $\mathcal{M}'$; and complexity bounds for the associated decision problems (e.g., checking whether a candidate $\mathcal{M}'$ is a maximally preserving repair).

